@@ -36,3 +36,13 @@ the whole target, and an object patch applied to a non-object target starts from
 format specifies, `null` therefore cannot be set as a member value, and arrays are replaced, not merged;
 use `applyPatch` when you need either. It never throws for JSON input, and only own members of the
 target are considered.
+
+### Creating a JSON Patch
+
+`createPatch(from, to)` returns a JSON Patch (RFC 6902) that turns `from` into `to` when passed to
+`applyPatch`, so clients can send only what changed. Objects are compared member by member and arrays
+element by element, so a small change deep in a document gives a single small operation, and equal
+documents give an empty patch. Only `add`, `remove` and `replace` are produced. When arrays differ in
+length, the surplus tail is removed (highest index first) or the new elements are appended with `-`;
+an element inserted at the front of an array is therefore described as changes to the following
+elements rather than as one `add`. Neither input is mutated, and the patch shares nothing with them.
